      en: "Maseru is the capital and largest city of Lesotho, situated on the banks of the Caledon River, which forms the international border with South Africa. Established as a small police camp in 1869 by the British, it was designated as the administrative capital of Basutoland following the conclusion of the Free State–Basotho Wars. The city sits at an elevation of approximately 1,600 meters above sea level, nestled at the foot of the Maloti Mountains. Today, Maseru serves as the political, economic, and transport hub of the kingdom, hosting major government buildings, the Royal Palace, and international embassies. Its architecture reflects a mix of colonial-era sandstone structures and modern commercial developments that have emerged since independence in 1966. The city's growth was significantly influenced by its proximity to the South African railway network and its role in the regional mohair and wool trade. Maseru remains the primary gateway for international visitors entering the mountain kingdom via the Maseru Bridge border post.",
